<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<raw:RawExtractionResult xmlns:xmi="http://www.omg.org/XMI" xmlns:raw="http://www.xaikg.org/1.0/raw" sourceDocument="bc5cdr/6615679" sourceUri="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6615679/" sourceSha256="ab794781609d726cb70aeaf21e0213342b4adffbd81080feabc92680a3d581f4" sourceText="Intraoperative bradycardia and hypotension associated with timolol and pilocarpine eye drops. A 69-yr-old man, who was concurrently being treated with pilocarpine nitrate and timolol maleate eye drops, developed a bradycardia and became hypotensive during halothane anaesthesia. Both timolol and pilocarpine were subsequently identified in a 24-h collection of urine. Timolol (but not pilocarpine) was detected in a sample of plasma removed during surgery; the plasma concentration of timolol (2.6 ng ml-1) was consistent with partial beta-adrenoceptor blockade. It is postulated that this action may have been enhanced during halothane anaesthesia with resultant bradycardia and hypotension. Pilocarpine may have had a contributory effect." extractionTask="drug_label_extraction" llmModel="gemini/gemini-3.1-flash-lite" promptId="drug_label_extraction@fc3d922aa277" promptHash="fc3d922aa27751cd23bc5136339e3e9669ebbfd16084612234d8ae7245fceda4" extractionTimestamp="2026-10-15T14:56:32+00:00" xmi:version="2.0">
